On Fri, 12 Dec 2003, David Gale wrote:
>
> "LesterMajesto" <lestermajesto@aol.com> wrote:
> > 2 points for *creative and reasonable* new armor/weapon/tool/artifact
> ideas
>
> How about a dowsing rod?  Similar to a crystal ball, it allows you to find
> things, but a) it's lighter, and b) it's shorter range.  I envision it being
> most useful for finding buried treasure, though I could also see it being
> useful for finding vaults (if the range is long enough) and such.
> Unidentified, it just looks like a stick.

  Given that there aren't any other "sticks" in the game, I don't
see much point in having a different un-IDed description for it.
Crystal balls are just crystal balls, right?  I think "dowsing rod"
works, although "forked stick" does look *right* for the un-IDed
version for some reason.  (No, re the "add a plain stick" suggestion,
I don't think it's worth adding yet another useless object to Nethack.
Flint stones, mirrors, glass, and tin whistles are useless enough for
me. :)
  [Yes, stones can be thrown, mirrors can be (a)pplied, and whistles
work like magic whistles only less so.  They're still useless as far
as any of my characters are concerned.  Point is, I don't like the
"plain stick" addition.]


> It'd be a charged item[1],
> probably with ~25 charges (not sure, but hey).  (a)pplying it would cause it
> to "tug" the player towards the nearest (non-held) object ("The dowsing rod
> tugs to the right!") if there's anything in range; otherwise, something like
> "You wave your [stick/dowsing rod] in the air."  If the player's standing on
> something that's buried, have it tug downwards.

  More dramatically, I would want to see the rod actually *move* the
player when applied successfully, if possible (that is, if there were
no wall, floor, ceiling, gravitational force, or monster blocking the
way).  This could obviously pose a serious danger to the player if
the rod turns out to be attracted to water...!

>  Cursed would tug in a
> random direction; blessed would have a better range, and maybe a
> predilection to tug towards valuable (high $ value) stuff.

  Cursed rods could also tug towards monsters, lava, stairs down,
traps, and other hazards.  Blessed rods might give the player the
ability to specify a particular feature or item type to detect.
  Alternatively, there might be several different kinds of dowsing
rods: "a water-dowsing rod," "a gold-dowsing rod," et cetera, which
would make the alternative un-IDed text important.

> Confused, the
> rod points towards the player, or some such.

  Or in a random direction, or "straight up," possibly banging your
head against the ceiling or causing damage in the fall...

> Of course, you could also add in underground water, which the dowsing rod
> would detect; digging in that spot would create a pool, with a small chance
> of creating a fountain/sink instead...

  Creating a sink by digging? :)


> [1] Alternative method of using: you (w)ield the rod and walk around with
> it, getting the tugs as you go.  In this instance, it wouldn't be charged,
> of course, and would do very little damage (regular sticks could do slightly
> more).  This is perhaps more in line with the way dowsers work in RL...I'm
> not sure which I prefer.  Thoughts?

  I like this idea better than the (a)pply idea, actually.  Let it
work more like a very-short-range ring of teleport.  In this case,
I think you'd have to have multiple types of rod, since the player
wouldn't be able to specify what he was trying to dowse for otherwise.
(The rod would have to be consistent between activations -- getting
a tug toward water, then toward gold, would be very confusing!)

  Also to consider: Would the rod tug along a straight path, or
use path-finding to pull you along existing corridors if possible?
Maybe *that* could be a BUC factor? although it might be another
case where some players would prefer the uncursed effect to the
blessed...

